From 55d89a99bf521a512990ab83e333b2189da88bcb Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: fmillion <47171880+fmillion@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 22 Nov 2026 05:36:32 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 06/42] drm/i915: Bind Lakefield (8086:9840) as a
 render/media-only Gen11 GT

i915 has no Lakefield platform. Its Gen11 GT behaves like Elkhart Lake's
(bcs0/rcs0/vcs0/vecs0, 36-bit PPGTT, sane fused frequency table), so bind
8086:9840 with ehl_info. With no display descriptor for the ID, display
initialisation is skipped and the firmware framebuffer keeps being
scanned out by simpledrm.

To coexist with simpledrm on the same PCI function, unless the new
i915.lkf_display parameter is set:
 - do not remove the conflicting firmware framebuffer,
 - reserve the GGTT range holding the BOOTFB (16 MiB at offset 0;
   1536x2048x4 = 12 MiB on the ThinkPad X1 Fold),
 - do not use stolen memory (it holds the BOOTFB),
 - keep runtime PM forbidden (D3 would freeze the scanout; the GT still
   enters RC6).

Also add i915.lkf_chipset_id to report a different ID through
I915_PARAM_CHIPSET_ID (e.g. 0x4e71) for userspace (intel-media-driver)
that does not know 0x9840 yet.

Based on the Galaxy Book S render-only work by franke7777
(github.com/franke7777/galaxy-book-s-linux).

Tested on a ThinkPad X1 Fold Gen 1: GLES via iris (with a 0x4e71 PCI ID
for Mesa) renders correctly; VA-API (iHD) H.264/HEVC/VP9 decode is
bit-exact with software decode; no GPU hangs.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
